        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What size dog treadmill do I need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Measure your dog from the tip of the nose to the base of the tail. A widely used rule of thumb is that the running area should be at least twice that length so the dog can extend fully at a trot or run. Then check that the weight rating sits comfortably above your dog's weight. If your dog is close to a limit, size up.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can my dog use my own treadmill instead?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some dogs can manage slow, supervised walks on a human treadmill, but it is a poorer fit: the belt is often short for a dog's stride, there is a motor hood at nose height, no side panels, and a safety key a dog cannot pull. Our treadmill for dogs guide covers when it can work and when it should not be used.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I tie or clip my dog to the treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A dog tied to a treadmill cannot step off if it stumbles, panics or tires, and the belt keeps moving. Some manuals and listings show a leash clipped to the frame, but our position is that a dog should always be free to step off. If a dog only stays on the belt when tethered, it is not ready for a treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a motorised dog treadmill or a slatmill better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the dog. A motorised treadmill lets you set the speed and duration, which suits most pet dogs and vet-directed programmes. A slatmill has no motor, so the dog sets the pace and the belt stops when the dog does, which suits high-drive working and sporting dogs. Slatmills generally cost more and take longer to learn.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a dog spend on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              That is a question for your vet, because it depends on the dog's age, breed, fitness and health. In general terms, introductions start with the belt stopped and then very short sessions at the lowest speed, built up gradually over days or weeks, with the session ended at any sign of distress, lagging or overheating. Never leave a dog unattended on a belt.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a dog treadmill a substitute for walks?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A treadmill can add exercise in bad weather, during restricted outdoor activity, or for a dog that needs more running than you can provide. It cannot replace the sniffing, new places, social contact and time with you that a walk gives. Most owners use one as a supplement, and for many dogs more walks are the better answer.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

